		}
		{
			dependencies: ["504BB1A0C0A5D778"]
			description: [
				"   The best catalyst in this chapter is a slime ball dipped in vegetable oil. Don't ask."
				""
				"   The &6Refinery&r turns &a500 mB&r of plant oil into &a250 mB&r of &6Aqueous Organic Solvent&r, and the &6Bottler&r coats a slime ball in &a50 mB&r of it. Out comes the &6Gelatinous Organic Solvent&r."
				""
				"   In the Pulverizer it's the strongest all-rounder by far: &a1.6×&r main output, &a2.25×&r by-products, &a15%&r energy so runs fly by, a &a50%&r floor, and it's only spent a quarter of the time. That's two Obsidianite per blob, on average. Rich Slag beats it on the floor and nothing else."
				""
				"   The catch is the slime. Plant oil is easy to keep flowing; slime balls have to come from somewhere, and this thing will eat a lot of them."
				""
				"&7&o— Smells like salad dressing. Performs like cheating.&r&r"
			]
			id: "0E15CFE7649C8E5E"
			shape: "gear"
			tasks: [{
				id: "77215FC08FA0171E"
				item: "kubejs:gelatinous_organic_solvent"
				type: "item"
			}]
			title: "Bio-Structural Catalyst"
			x: -5.0d
			y: 1.0d
		}
		{
			dependencies: [
				"4807891B99079DE0"
				"3321B64AAB3C71A1"
			]
			description: [
				"   &6Cinnabar&r is quartz that the &6Bottler&r soaked in &a500 mB&r of molten redstone, which the &6Crucible&r melts out of plain redstone dust. Two machines you just built, one red crystal. Not that you need them: it also shows up as ore underground, and the Pulverizer already spits it out of redstone ore a quarter of the time."
				""
				"   It doesn't excel at anything. &a1.25×&r on both main output and by-products, &a80%&r energy, a &a25%&r floor, and it survives &a70%&r of runs. That last number is the one that matters: this is the catalyst you leave in the slot and stop thinking about. For Obsidianite it costs a little over one crystal per gem, which makes Quartz Dust look like a bad joke."
				""
				"&7&o— Real cinnabar is mercury ore. Ours is redstone with ambitions, so technically you can lick it. Please don't.&r&r"
			]
			id: "1F6EBFB9DB2A2B14"
			shape: "gear"
			tasks: [{
				id: "6D2A60FB21D90FE1"
				item: "thermal:cinnabar"
				type: "item"
			}]
			title: "Red Crystal Reagent"
			x: -5.0d
			y: -0.5d
		}
		{
			dependencies: ["4B17B25AEC9A0007"]
			description: [
				"   An &6Ice Charge&r is &a500 mB&r of water and one gunpowder, frozen together in the &6Chiller&r. In the Pulverizer it buys speed and little else: &a30%&r energy per run, a &a15%&r floor, by-products untouched, and main output actually drops to &c0.9×&r. It survives one run in twenty."
				""
				"   Coke Dust gives the same speed, doesn't shave your dust and lasts longer. So this goes in the slot when the coke runs out, or when a pile of something cheap needs grinding right now and losing a tenth of it doesn't hurt."
				""
				"&7&o— You froze an explosive so a rock grinder spins faster. I'd have said no, but it works.&r&r"
			]
			id: "1DB47027209C4091"
			shape: "gear"
			tasks: [{
				id: "418CDA44CA87730C"
				item: "thermal:ice_charge"
				type: "item"
			}]
			title: "Directed Thermal Shock"
			x: -5.0d
			y: 2.5d
		}
		{
			dependencies: ["42B368C1DD78B214"]
			description: [
				"   &6Rich Slag&r doesn't make anything faster. Energy stays at 100% and main output barely moves (1.05×). What it has is the floor: &a60%&r, the highest of any catalyst, plus &a1.75×&r by-products, and it's spent only &a20%&r of the time."
				""
				"   That makes it the one for the 0% recipes. Three Obsidianite per slag, on average, and rutile and ilmenite drop more often than not. Coke Dust clears a backlog; this one squeezes the rare stuff out of every cluster."
				""
				"   It comes from the &6Pyrolyzer&r: regular slag in, Rich Slag out 25% of the time, tar on the side. Regular slag is the leftover of plenty of smelting recipes, so if a pile of it has been sitting in a chest, now it has a purpose."
				""
				"&7&o— The smelter's garbage, cooked twice, becomes the most valuable thing in the slot. I'd make a speech, but you're busy grinding.&r&r"
			]
			id: "41F84F8BA400D58F"
			shape: "gear"
			tasks: [{
				id: "4C1F251E95EB52D8"
				item: "thermal:rich_slag"
				type: "item"
			}]
			title: "Reprocessed Purity Residue"
			x: -5.0d
			y: 4.0d
		}
		{
			dependencies: ["5697C0E23B256642"]
			description: [
				"   Given too much stimulus, even a plant yields. In the Phytogenic Insolator, under constant light, pure water, and excess Phyto-Gro, it tries to protect itself. It closes its stomata. Reinforces its fibers. Stiffens its cell walls until it becomes… something else. Something dead."
				""
				"   Instead of blooming, it forms a carapace of dense cellulose and modified lignin — a defense reaction that, ironically, suffocates it. Without noticing, the plant literally embalms itself to death, an “auto-immune disease.” The result is Organic Polymer Mesh: a high-performance plant polymer, flexible, heat-resistant, and completely inert. Perfect for machines. Horrible for photosynthesis."
				""
				"   Curious how we chase dense, rare metals only to find the chapter’s most useful material comes from plants and water. Don’t underestimate &2&lgreen engineering!&r&r"
				""
				"&7&o— Fascinating. Give a plant paradise and it embalms itself.&r&r"
			]
			id: "1E7A8B399C39BF92"
			shape: "hexagon"
			size: 1.5d
			tasks: [{
				id: "7B017C8ACAC68D96"
				item: "kubejs:organic_polymer_mesh"
				type: "item"
			}]
			x: -2.5d
			y: 6.0d
		}
